  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They took control in one inning and never looked back.",
    "what_this_means": "Cole was sharp for six, giving up four hits and two runs while striking out six. Yarbrough, Fulmer, and Gil combined to shut Arizona out for the final three innings. The offense didn't need much. Five runs in the first, two more in the seventh, and that was the game. Lombard drove in three of them. By the time the fifth inning arrived, this was already decided. Arizona scored twice early but had no answer after that. The Yankees built the lead wire-to-wire and held it cold. A 9\u20132 final looks dominant because it was.",
    "what_to_watch": "This is what a balanced game looks like when both sides click. The starter established control early, the bullpen held its lane clean, and the offense converted runners into runs instead of leaving them stranded. Lombard's three RBIs mattered because the lineup got runners on. Eight hits total meant opportunities, and they converted five of them in scoring position. The pitching side did not require drama. Cole gave them six solid innings, the bridge was seamless, and the closer locked it down. No one thing carried this game. The alignment held, the formula worked, and the result was a clean win in a spot where division position matters. They won because everything worked. That doesn't happen often.",
